{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC renderer robustness & performance hardening\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe DC path shells out to pandoc per field, which introduces three production\nhazards proven in the DC experiment: (1) a per-field subprocess tax — latency is a\nflat ~0.3s/field spawn cost independent of body size, and a serial reconcile pass\nover many fields blocked ~20 min of wall time on `fork+exec`; (2) a\npathological-input hang — one corpus body drove pandoc's jira reader to spin\n13.5 min at 95.8% CPU, and pypandoc invokes pandoc with no timeout, so one field\ncan stall the whole reconcile indefinitely; (3) pandoc is GPL and rides in the\n`pypandoc-binary` wheel, so its provenance and license must be pinned and recorded.\nThese must be designed against before the DC codec ships live.\n\n## Prior art / dependency boundaries (do NOT re-decide what 271c already chose)\n- **The renderer module and its pandoc delivery are owned by 271c.** Story\n  `[rebar:271c-e88f-3220-4c9b]` ships the pure renderer at\n  `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` and delivers pandoc via the\n  **`pypandoc-binary` wheel** (the pandoc binary is BUNDLED IN THE WHEEL and pinned\n  in `pyproject.toml`), specifically so the DC path is self-contained in CI and\n  prod with no system pandoc install. This story hardens THAT module and pins THAT\n  wheel; it does **not** introduce a separate system-level pandoc or an\n  `acli`-style version+sha binary pin (acli is a symlink + `acli jira auth login`\n  system prerequisite, not a pinned pip binary — there is no acli pin pattern to\n  mirror).\n- 271c leaves `WikiTextCodec` fully identity and delivers `wiki_render.py` as a\n  **pure module not referenced by any codec op or live send path in that story**\n  (271c's own words); all live wiring — the codec ops AND the live send sites — is\n  deferred to the cutover story `[rebar:3388-96e8-e14e-4221]` and is **out of scope\n  here**. This story touches only the pure `wiki_render.py` module functions, never\n  the codec or the live send.\n- **The change-gated \"no-op → no pandoc spawn\" property is delivered by the\n  cutover/echo story `[rebar:3388-96e8-e14e-4221]`**, which introduces the\n  change-gated `rich_sha` render gate; that symbol does not yet exist on\n  `origin/main` and is not this story's to add. THIS story owns only the cost,\n  timeout, fallback, batching, and compliance of a render that IS performed.\n\n## Approach\n- **Bound the per-render subprocess cost by batching contiguous SAFE units:** 271c\n  segments the body into units and classifies each safe or dangerous. This story\n  groups each **maximal run of contiguous SAFE units** and feeds that run to pandoc\n  as **one Markdown document → one wiki segment**, so a body of many safe blocks\n  costs one spawn per safe-run instead of one per unit. Crucially there is **no\n  per-unit re-splitting of a batch's output**: the safe-run's combined wiki is\n  emitted as a single contiguous segment, and reassembly is at the segment (run)\n  level, not the unit level. (pandoc 3.9 exposes no `--server`/daemon, confirmed, so\n  a persistent server is out of scope; safe-run batching is the available lever.)\n- **Dangerous units are spliced VERBATIM — pandoc is never invoked on them.** Per\n  271c, a unit flagged dangerous corrupts pandoc's jira reader, so it is passed\n  through as its original Markdown **without any pandoc call** and inserted at its\n  original position between the rendered safe-run segments. Original order is\n  restored by concatenating the ordered sequence of segments (rendered safe-run wiki\n  chunks and verbatim dangerous units) — no delimiter/sentinel splitting is needed\n  because each pandoc invocation renders exactly one contiguous safe run.\n- **Hard timeout + process-group reaping + batch-level verbatim fallback:** a plain\n  `subprocess.run(timeout=)` / pypandoc timeout only reaps the DIRECT child, so a\n  pandoc jira-reader spinning at ~95.8% CPU (a pipe-holding grandchild) survives and\n  keeps burning the core (repo bug d843, CPython bpo-30154). Therefore the renderer\n  MUST NOT use pypandoc's high-level `convert_text`/`convert_file` API (which spawns\n  pandoc internally and returns no handle); instead it locates the wheel-bundled\n  pandoc executable via `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()` and builds its OWN\n  `subprocess.Popen([pandoc_path, …], start_new_session=True)`, **enforces the\n  wall-clock timeout caller-side via `proc.communicate(timeout=dc_pandoc_timeout_s)`\n  and, on `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, passes that `Popen`** to the shared leaf\n  **`rebar._proc.reap_process_group`** (`_proc.py:26`) —\n  `killpg` SIGTERM→grace→SIGKILL→bounded drain, the exact helper already reused by\n  the ACLI transport and grounding harness — so the whole process group, not just\n  the direct child, is killed on timeout. The reap helper only kills/drains AFTER a\n  timeout is detected; it does not itself wait out the timeout, so the caller-side\n  `communicate(timeout=…)`/`except TimeoutExpired` detection is required, exactly the\n  pattern `acli_subprocess.py` uses. **The wall-clock timeout is a config key**\n  (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default **10s**) chosen from the DC experiment:\n  it is >30× the observed ~0.3s/field normal render (so it never trips healthy\n  bodies) and ~80× below the 13.5-min pathological hang (so a spinning field is\n  killed in seconds, not minutes); `grace`/`drain` reuse the ACLI caller's existing\n  constants. On a safe-run pandoc call's timeout or\n  non-zero exit, degrade **that entire safe-run segment** to its original Markdown\n  verbatim (plain Markdown is trivially echo-safe) and record a **segment-level\n  fallback marker**; other safe-run segments and the verbatim dangerous units are\n  unaffected. The fallback boundary is exactly the pandoc-invocation boundary (one\n  contiguous safe run), so there is no partial-segment reassembly to reconcile.\n- **Compliance:** pin the `pypandoc-binary` wheel by version in `pyproject.toml`\n  (271c's mechanism), record the wheel-bundled pandoc's GPL license in the project\n  SBOM/notice, and verify the wheel pin in CI.\n\n## Scope\n- `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` — the pure DC renderer module delivered by\n  271c: locate the wheel-bundled pandoc via `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()` and drive\n  it through the renderer's OWN `subprocess.Popen(start_new_session=True)` (NOT\n  pypandoc's high-level convert API), add the per-invocation timeout with\n  process-group reaping via the shared `rebar._proc.reap_process_group`,\n  contiguous-safe-run batching (one pandoc call per safe run → one wiki segment;\n  dangerous units spliced verbatim with NO pandoc call), and segment-level verbatim\n  fallback. No codec op and no live-send site is touched here.\n- `pyproject.toml` — pin the `pypandoc-binary` wheel version (extends 271c's pin;\n  no separate system pandoc).\n- config (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default 10s) — the per-invocation\n  wall-clock timeout, tunable without a code change. Because a `reconciler.*` key\n  has a fixed multi-file config surface in this repo (mirrors the existing\n  `reconciler.jira_cli_timeout`), ALL of the following are updated together for the\n  key to be reachable and documented: `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — add the\n  field `dc_pandoc_timeout_s: float = 10.0` to `ReconcilerConfig` plus its validator\n  entry `_as_float(v, k, minimum=0.0)` alongside `jira_cli_timeout`;\n  `src/rebar/_config_sources.py` — a canonical-env alias in `_CANONICAL_ENV_NAMES`\n  only if a non-derived env name is wanted (otherwise the auto-derived\n  `REBAR_RECONCILER_DC_PANDOC_TIMEOUT_S` applies and no entry is added);\n  `tests/golden/config_surface.json` — deliberately REGENERATE the golden to add the\n  `reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s` entry (the golden guard\n  `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py` is removal-only — it will NOT flag a new\n  live key's omission, so the surface is regenerated on purpose when the key is\n  added); `docs/config.md` — document the key in the `[tool.rebar.reconciler]` block.\n  A unit test mirrors the existing `test_config_file_wiring.py` four-consumer pattern\n  for the new key.\n- CI workflow config — verify the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pin (NOT an acli-style\n  system-binary sha check).\n- SBOM/licensing notice file — record the wheel-bundled pandoc GPL entry.\n- `tests/` — timeout, batch-fallback, and batching tests over the vendored DC\n  corpus fixture.\n\n## Testing\n- Timeout path: a synthetic input that exceeds the configured timeout is killed by\n  process-group reaping (the CPU-spinning pandoc grandchild is gone, not just the\n  direct child) and its safe-run segment falls back to verbatim Markdown while the\n  render returns; asserted by `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` (spawns a\n  child that forks a spinning grandchild; asserts the group PID is reaped).\n- Failure path: a non-zero pandoc exit degrades that safe-run segment to plaintext\n  without raising, and the segment fallback is recorded; `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades`.\n- Batching + order: a body of N contiguous safe units renders in one pandoc\n  invocation (spawn-count spy) and the output preserves original block order when\n  interleaved with verbatim dangerous units; `test_dc_render_batches_safe_run`.\n- Dangerous units never hit pandoc: a body containing a dangerous unit invokes\n  pandoc only on the safe runs and passes the dangerous unit through verbatim (the\n  spawn-count spy records zero pandoc calls for the dangerous unit);\n  `test_dc_dangerous_unit_verbatim_no_pandoc`.\n- Segment==fallback boundary: when one safe-run invocation times out, exactly that\n  run's segment falls back to verbatim and other safe runs still render;\n  `test_dc_segment_fallback_boundary`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every pandoc invocation runs under a wall-clock timeout enforced caller-side\n  via `proc.communicate(timeout=dc_pandoc_timeout_s)` (config\n  `reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default 10s — well above the ~0.3s normal render,\n  well below the 13.5-min pathological hang) and, on `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`,\n  process-group reaping (`rebar._proc.reap_process_group`, which kills/drains AFTER\n  the caller detects the timeout, so a pipe-holding CPU-spinning grandchild is\n  killed, not orphaned) and, on timeout or non-zero exit, degrades\n  that safe-run segment to verbatim Markdown without raising; the render returns;\n  verified by `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` (asserts the spawned group is\n  reaped) and `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades`.\n- [ ] A changed body of N contiguous safe units renders in one pandoc invocation\n  (one wiki segment) with original block order preserved, dangerous units are\n  spliced VERBATIM with no pandoc call, and a safe-run timeout degrades exactly that\n  run's segment; verified by `test_dc_render_batches_safe_run`,\n  `test_dc_dangerous_unit_verbatim_no_pandoc`, and `test_dc_segment_fallback_boundary`\n  (segment boundary == fallback boundary).\n- [ ] The pandoc binary is provided by the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pinned by\n  version in `pyproject.toml` (271c's mechanism, not a system pandoc), the wheel\n  pin is verified in CI, and the wheel-bundled pandoc GPL license is recorded in\n  the SBOM/notice; verified by the CI wheel-pin check and a notice-file test."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785818121275586001,"uuid":"738e165a-627d-410e-aa87-01922d6b73da"}